csv:     table-6-1-chemical-requirements.csv
source:  ../source.pdf                        # table 6-1 = source.pdf p.11; sect. 6-14 = p.10
anchor:  Prevailing Shell Steel Specifications  # sect. 6-14 heading, the section that introduces the table

# Ammunition Series 6 (17 Feb 1953), table 6-1 -- chemical requirements of the
# War Department Shell Steels.  Six grades (WDSS 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7 -- the source
# prints no WDSS 4) x five elements, all figures PERCENT BY WEIGHT.
#
# Column pairs are <element>_lo,<element>_hi.  A BLANK _lo means the source
# states a ceiling only ("0.60 max."), with no floor.  Do not read those blanks
# as zero: "0.60 max." and "0.00-0.60" are different claims, and only the first
# is on the page.  Digits are the page's own -- "0.040", not "0.04" -- because
# in a specification the trailing zero IS the stated precision.
#
# NO ARITHMETIC CLOSURE INVARIANT EXISTS FOR THIS TABLE, and that is a property
# of the table, not an omission.  .claude/rules/source-data-fidelity.md asks for
# a relation internal to the table that must hold on every row; a composition
# spec has none.  Its cells are independent limits set by metallurgical policy,
# not quantities related by arithmetic -- nothing sums, nothing is monotonic
# down a column (the grades are not ordered by any of these elements), and no
# stated criterion closes on the rows.  Under that rule the table would
# therefore be flagged for human review rather than passed.
#
# WHAT SUBSTITUTES, AND WHY IT IS STRONGER.  This scan carries a clean embedded
# text layer that renders the table cell-for-cell, so fidelity is settled here
# by EXACT MACHINE COMPARISON against the page rather than inferred from
# arithmetic.  This CSV was written BY that comparison script's --emit mode,
# through the same parser it verifies with, so no digit in it was hand-typed:
#
#   uv run python experiment/fragmentation-field/challenges/source-data-audit/
#     checks/ammunition-series-6-table-6-1-fidelity.py
#
# Run it before citing this table -- the one check below is far too weak alone.
# A closure is a proxy for "was the right row and column read"; a positional
# text-layer diff answers that question directly, and answers it for every cell
# rather than for the aggregate.
#
# THE ONE INTERNAL CHECK THAT DOES EXIST.  The phosphorus ceiling is constant
# down all six rows, so a slipped digit or a row/column shift in that column
# fails here.  It says nothing about the other four columns.
row:  p_hi == 0.040 within 0.001

# NOT A CLOSURE, BUT WORTH KNOWING: sect. 6-14's prose says "All shell steel is
# made by the basic open-hearth process to fine grain practice, silicon 0.15 to
# 0.30 percent", which matches si_lo/si_hi on five of the six rows and
# CONTRADICTS WDSS 1's tabulated "0.10 max.".  That disagreement is in the
# source itself -- both readings were confirmed against the text layer -- so it
# must not be "fixed" in this CSV.  See card.md, "Internal inconsistencies".
